{
  "dom_id": "step-q54245",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "A triangle $△ABC$ has $m∠A={37}^{∘},$ $m∠B={90}^{∘},$ and $AC=25.$ Find $AB$ rounded to the nearest integer.",
  "worked_solution": "For an angle $\\theta $ in a right triangle, the trigonometric ratios are as follows:\n\n- $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{adjacent leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}$\n\n- $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite leg}}{\\text{adjacent leg}}$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the given triangle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14203245/assets/70ac3cbbc7209f71bd8095bb725d1dbb847c827bdfdc8220c25115fb120532b4.png)\n\nFirst, note that we are given the length of the hypotenuse $\\overset{―}{AC}$ and wish to find the length of the leg $\\overset{―}{AB}$ adjacent to the given angle $∠A.$\n\nThe trigonometric ratio that relates the adjacent leg to the hypotenuse is *cosine*. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(m∠A) & =\\frac{\\text{adjacent leg}}{\\text{hypotenuse}} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡({37}^{∘}) & =\\frac{AB}{25} \\\\ AB & =25\\operatorname{cos}⁡({37}^{∘}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing a calculator to evaluate the trigonometric ratio, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =25\\operatorname{cos}⁡({37}^{∘}) \\\\  & =25(0.798\\,63…) \\\\  & =19.965\\,88… \\\\  & ≈20,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
